==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌwaɪt ˈnɔɪz/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-white noise.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}
{{listen|White noise.ogg|Ten seconds of '''white noise'''}}

# {{senseid|en|Q381287}}{{lb|en|physics}} A [[random]] [[signal]] (or [[process]]) with a [[flat]] [[power]] [[spectral]] [[density]]; a signal with a power spectral density that has equal power in any [[band]], at any centre frequency, having a given bandwidth.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=John G. Holden|title=Gauging the Fractal Dimension of Response Times from Cognitive Tasks|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101225100253/http://www.nsf.gov/sbe/bcs/pac/nmbs/chap6.pdf
|passage=Conventional statistical analyses presuppose that intrinsic variability is '''white noise'''. '''White noise''' yields a jagged and irregular line with a fractal dimension of 1.5, because successive observations are statistically independent of each other.}}
# {{lb|en|[[nontechnically]]}} Any [[nondescript]] [[noise]] used for [[background]] or to [[mask]] or [[drown out]] other noise.
# {{lb|en|figuratively|by extension}} [[speech|Speech]] that goes [[ignored]] or [[uncomprehended]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=C. R. Robertson|title=Elements of Fae|page=137
|passage=The rest was '''white noise'''; I was sick of listening to who I was supposed to be.}}

